---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import DotGridDivisions from "../../../components/DotGridDivisions.astro";
import DotCountPicker from "../../../components/DotCountPicker.astro";
import DotStrip from "../../../components/DotStrip.astro";
import NamedLayoutTable from "../../../components/NamedLayoutTable.astro";
import {
  DOT_COUNTS,
  MAX_DOTS,
  MIN_DOTS,
  SPACING_MM,
  cellsFor,
  millimetres,
  spaceLayouts,
} from "../../../references/dot-grid/dot-grid";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return DOT_COUNTS.map((dots) => ({ params: { dots: String(dots) } }));
}

const dots = Number(Astro.params.dots);
const cells = cellsFor(dots);
const spreadCells = cells * 2;

// Both contexts offer every layout. Which axis a spread doubles depends on how the
// notebook is held, so nothing here is filtered by axis.
const pageLayouts = spaceLayouts(cells);
const spreadLayouts = spaceLayouts(spreadCells);


---

<Layout
  title={`${dots} dots — Nico Bako`}
  description={`How to divide a run of ${dots} dots (${cells} cells) on a dot grid — equally, as columns on a page or spread, and as rows.`}
>
  <Breadcrumbs
    trail={[
      { label: "References", href: "/references/" },
      { label: "Dot Grid", href: "/references/dot-grid/" },
    ]}
  />

  <h1>{dots} dots</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    A run of <b>{dots} dots</b> is <b>{cells} cells</b> — you write between the
    dots, not on them, so there is always one fewer cell than dot. At {SPACING_MM} mm
    spacing that is {millimetres(cells)} mm of paper.
  </p>

  <DotCountPicker current={dots} />

  <p>
    Which edge you counted is up to you: dividing a run into equal parts is the
    same arithmetic across the page or down it, and every layout below works
    either way round. Each is drawn as a run left to right, so read a strip as
    columns or as bands stacked down the page, whichever you are laying out.
  </p>

  <h2>On a single page — {cells} cells</h2>

  <h3>Divide equally</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Spare</b> is what will not fit: split it between the two ends as margin,
    or keep it together as a sidebar.
  </p>
  <DotGridDivisions cells={cells} caption={`A run of ${cells} cells`} />

  <h3>Named layouts</h3>
  <NamedLayoutTable cells={cells} />

  <h3>Layouts that are not evenly divided</h3>
  <p class="lead">
    An equal split is the obvious answer and often the wrong one: a label and the
    text beside it do not want the same width. The filled block holds the
    content.
  </p>
  <div class="layouts">
    {
      pageLayouts.map((layout) => (
        <article>
          <h4>{layout.name}</h4>
          <DotStrip segments={layout.segments} labelled />
          <p class="sizes">
            {`${layout.segments.map((segment) => segment.cells).join(" · ")} cells`}
          </p>
          <p>{layout.why}</p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Across a spread — {spreadCells} cells</h2>
  <p>
    Two pages joined at the spine. Only one of the two axes doubles — the one
    that crosses the spine — and which one that is depends on how you are
    holding the notebook: upright, the run across the page doubles and the rows
    are untouched; turned ninety degrees, it is the other way round. The axis
    that does not cross the spine is unchanged, and the tables above are still
    its answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fold falls exactly in the middle, which is worth planning around: an
    <b>even</b> number of parts puts a boundary on it, so the fold does the
    dividing for free and each page holds a whole number of parts. An odd number
    puts a part across the fold — fine for a table you read across, awkward for
    one you write in.
  </p>

  <h3>Divide equally</h3>
  <DotGridDivisions cells={spreadCells} caption={`A spread of ${spreadCells} cells`} />

  <h3>Named layouts</h3>
  <NamedLayoutTable cells={spreadCells} />

  <h3>Layouts that are not evenly divided</h3>
  <p class="lead">
    The proportions hold, but the narrow parts get room to breathe: a gutter
    stays a gutter while the content part doubles.
  </p>
  <div class="layouts">
    {
      spreadLayouts.map((layout) => (
        <article>
          <h4>{layout.name}</h4>
          <DotStrip segments={layout.segments} labelled />
          <p class="sizes">
            {`${layout.segments.map((segment) => segment.cells).join(" · ")} cells`}
          </p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="footnote">
    Counted <i>squares</i> rather than dots? Then you want{" "}
    {dots + 1 <= MAX_DOTS ? (
      <a href={`/references/dot-grid/${dots + 1}`}>{dots + 1} dots</a>
    ) : (
      <>the next count up</>
    )}
    , since {dots} squares means {dots + 1} dots. Counts run from {MIN_DOTS} to{" "}
    {MAX_DOTS}.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  h3 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  h4:not(.layouts h4) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .layouts {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .layouts h4 {
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .layouts p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }


  .sizes {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .footnote {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
